From 94117355eddb6a166077d9ff4365f975a973ae83 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rustcc-build <rustcc@localhost>
Date: Wed, 3 Jun 2026 09:42:13 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 38/39] P09.x (1.13.7): cross-boundary virtual destructors for
 Rust subclasses
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

When a Rust `class D : CppBase` subclasses an imported C++ base with a
virtual destructor, `delete (CppBase*)d` from C++ now runs the Rust
`Drop`, destroys the base subobject, and frees — exactly once.

- The imported base's `#[rustc_cxx_imported_vtable]` carries `vdtor=1`
  (it declares a virtual dtor). `chain_has_virtual_dtor` walks the base
  chain for this marker.
- `emit_vtable` prepends the two leading Itanium dtor slots (complete
  `D1`, deleting `D0`) of the most-derived class. Both run the full Rust
  drop glue `drop_in_place::<D>` — which runs `Drop::drop` AND drops every
  field, including the `__base` subobject whose own `Drop` runs the C++
  base destructor (the bare `_ZN<D>D1Ev` symbol is only the `Drop::drop`
  body and would leak the base). The `D0` slot is a thunk that runs
  `drop_in_place::<D>` then `operator delete` (`_ZdlPv` / MSVC
  `??3@YAXPEAX@Z`), pairing with a factory that allocates via
  `operator new`.
- The mono collector now collects `drop_in_place::<D>` as a root for a
  `#[repr(cpp)]` class in a virtual-dtor chain (the vtable's reference is
  invisible to lazy collection).

Verified end-to-end (counters): base ctor/dtor balanced at 1, Rust Drop
runs once, no leak or double-free.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
